problems.problem:
Prove that:

a) no prime can be represented as the sum of two or more consecutive odd positive integers;

b) the square of any prime can be represented as the sum of two or more consecutive odd positive integers in one and only one way.

On an infinite square grid, Jaak and Jüri play the following game:

Jaak has $n$ red counters and Jüri one blue counter, which at the beginning of the game are on different squares of the board;

Jaak's move consists of moving one red counter by three squares (not necessarily in a straight line), where from a square one may only move to a square sharing a side with it;

Jüri's move consists of moving the blue counter in the same way by one square;

the game starts with Jaak's move and ends when, at the end of Jaak's move, some red counter is on the same square as the blue counter.

Find the smallest value of $n$ for which Jaak can end the game from any initial position.

The numbers $0, 1, 2, \dots, 9$ are written (in some order) on the circumference. Prove that

a) there are three consecutive numbers with the sum being at least $15$;

b) it is not necessarily the case that there exist three consecutive numbers with the sum more than $15$.

Call a positive integer lonely if the sum of reciprocals of its divisors (including $1$ and the integer itself) is not equal to the sum of reciprocals of divisors of any other positive integer. Prove that

a) all primes are lonely;

b) there exist infinitely many non-lonely positive integers.

On a lottery ticket a player has to mark $6$ numbers from $36$. Then $6$ numbers from these $36$ are drawn randomly and the ticket wins if none of the numbers that came out is marked on the ticket. Prove that

a) it is possible to mark the numbers on $9$ tickets so that one of these tickets always wins;

b) it is not possible to mark the numbers on $8$ tickets so that one of tickets always wins.

Let $n$ and $c$ be coprime positive integers. For any integer $i$, denote by $i'$ the remainder of division of product $ci$ by $n$. Let $A_0A_1 \ldots A_{n-1}$ be a regular $n$-gon. Prove that

a) if $A_iA_j \parallel A_kA_l$ then $A_{i'}A_{j'} \parallel A_{k'}A_{l'}$;

b) if $A_iA_j \perp A_kA_l$ then $A_{i'}A_{j'} \perp A_{k'}A_{l'}$.

A sequence of natural numbers $a_1, a_2, a_3, \dots$ is called periodic modulo $n$ if there exists a positive integer $k$ such that, for any positive integer $i$, the terms $a_i$ and $a_{i+k}$ are equal modulo $n$. Does there exist a strictly increasing sequence of natural numbers that

a) is not periodic modulo finitely many positive integers and is periodic modulo all the other positive integers;

b) is not periodic modulo infinitely many positive integers and is periodic modulo infinitely many positive integers?

Does there exist an equilateral triangle

(a) on a plane;

(b) in a 3-dimensional space;

such that all its three vertices have integral coordinates?

Does there exist a geometric progression, among the members of which there are

a) $3$, $45$ and $2025$;

b) $3$, $\frac{45}{\sqrt[3]{5}}$ and $2025$?
